# Scene Planner → Layout Mapping

How a Scene Planner scene object turns into one of the layouts from
`02_layout_catalogue.md`.

## Example input

```json
{
  "video_id": "...",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_1",
      "layout": "text_explanation",
      "script_segments": ["seg_1"],
      "visual_elements": [
        {
          "cue_id": "cue_1",
          "element_type": "heading",
          "content": "What is LangChain?",
          "asset_id": null,
          "linked_segment_id": "seg_1",
          "appearance_trigger": {"type": "on_word", "word_id": "word_1"},
          "preferred_region": "top",
          "importance": "primary"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Step 1 — Pick the layout

The `layout` field has to match one of the 8 catalogue IDs exactly: `title_intro`,
`narrator_note`, `text_explanation`, `image_media`, `comparison`, `step_by_step`,
`key_takeaway`, `closing_cta`. If it's empty or doesn't match anything, default to
`text_explanation` and log a warning.

## Step 2 — Map elements to fields

Scene Planner sends a flat list of `visual_elements`, each with an `element_type`. Here's how
those map onto the fields defined in `03_layout_specifications.md`:

| `element_type` | Maps to |
|---|---|
| `heading` | `title` / `heading` |
| `body` | `body_text` / `statement_text` / `takeaway_text` / `closing_message`, depending on the active layout |
| `subtitle` | `subtitle` (title_intro only) |
| `image` / `diagram` / `code_snippet` | `media_asset` / `background_media` |
| `caption` | `caption` (image_media only) |
| `icon` | `supporting_icon` / `avatar_icon` |
| `attribution` | `attribution_label` (narrator_note only) |
| `list_item` | `left_item.points[]` / `right_item.points[]` (comparison only, `preferred_region` says which side) |
| `step` | `steps[]` (step_by_step only, ordered by array position) |
| `cta` | `cta_text` (closing_cta only) |
| `logo` | `logo` (closing_cta only) |

Anything with an `element_type` we don't recognize just gets skipped, with a warning logged —
one bad element shouldn't take down the whole scene.

## `preferred_region`

This is a hint from the Scene Planner about where content should go. If it conflicts with
where the layout normally places that field, the layout wins — we want scenes to look
consistent from video to video, not shift around based on a hint. Log a warning when this
happens so it's visible for later.

## `importance`

If a scene has more content than the layout can fit, drop or trim `secondary` elements first.
If it's still over budget, trim `primary` elements too (using the layout's truncation rule).
Required fields never get dropped outright — they get truncated instead if there's no other
option.

## `linked_segment_id`

This ties a visual element to a script segment and eventually to timestamps from the
alignment stage. This lane doesn't use the timestamps directly — that's the sync/animation
stage's job — but every layout field here needs to pass `linked_segment_id` and
`appearance_trigger` through unchanged so sync is still possible downstream.